𐀀𐀟𐀈𐀐:
===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|gmy|𐀀𐀢𐀈𐀐||[[Appendix:Glossary#augment|unaugmented]]|ts1=apudōke}}

===Etymology===
From {{af|gmy|𐀀𐀢|*𐀇𐀈𐀁|t1=from|t2=to give}}.

Cognate with {{cog|grc|ἀπέδωκε}}, the third-person singular aorist active indicative of {{m|grc|ἀποδίδωμι}}.

===Verb===
{{head|gmy|verb|ts=apedōke}}

# [[delivered]], [[repaid]]
#* {{Q|gmy||{{w|Pylos}} PY Fr 1184|year=c. 1200 BCE|quote={{gmy-table|a={{m|gmy|𐀒𐀏𐀫|tr=-}}𐄁{{m-self|gmy|𐀀𐀟𐀈𐀐|tr=-}}𐄁{{m|gmy|𐀁𐁉𐀺|tr=-}}𐄁{{m|gmy|𐀵𐀰|tr=-}}|b={{m|gmy|𐀁𐀄𐀕𐀆𐀂|tr=-}}}}|tr={{gmy-table|a=ko-ka-ro '''a-pe-do-ke''' e-ra₃-wo to-so|b=e-u-me-de-i}}|ts=Kōkalos '''apedōke''' elaiwon tos(s)on Eumēdehi|trans=Kokalos '''repaid''' the following quantity of olive-oil to Eumedes.|transauthor={{w|Michael Ventris}}; {{w|John Chadwick}}|transyear=1956}}

====Usage notes====
The form {{m-self|gmy|𐀀𐀟𐀈𐀐|tr=-}} is usually interpreted as {{lang|gmy|sc=Latn|/apedōke/}}, the third-person singular aorist active indicative of {{m|gmy|*𐀀𐀢𐀇𐀈𐀁|ts=apudidohen}}. If this interpretation is correct, it is one of the rare attestations of the syllabic augment in Mycenaean Greek; preterite forms in the Linear B documents are normally unaugmented.

The alternative reading {{lang|gmy|sc=Latn|/apesdōke/}} has been proposed, analyzing the form without an augment and with an additional preverb {{m|gmy||*es-}}. This interpretation is generally considered less likely because compounds with two preverbs are extremely rare in Mycenaean, whereas forms belonging to the {{m|gmy|*𐀀𐀢𐀇𐀈𐀁|tr=-|ts=apudidohen}} word-family are well attested.

====Related terms====
* {{l|gmy|𐀀𐀢𐀈𐀯|ts=apudosis}}

===References===
